  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 132 PM EDT Sun Oct 4 2026\n\nPattern Synopsis/Forecast:\n\nShortwave trough and attendant rather sharp cold font now off to our \neast, although broader overhead upper level troughing remains. \nRather widespread showers from earlier have exited east with the \nfront, though cold air within trough axis is kicking off some lake \nclouds. Cold air advection on the heels of some gusty northwest \nwinds partially offsetting the normal diurnal temperature cycle, \nwith current readings in the 50s and 60s.\n\nTroughing will continue to deepen across the eastern Great Lakes \nthrough tonight, punting further off to our east on Monday. Surface \nhigh pressure will continue east as well, cutting across our area \nMonday into Monday evening. Progressive northern stream flow looks \nto continue thereafter through the remainder of next week, likely \nlocking any real chilly air to our north and keeping any tropical \nmoisture connection well to our south.\n\nPrimary Forecast Concerns:\n\nTemperature trends, with special emphasis on just how cold we get \nMonday night. Secondary focus on low end precipitation chances \nthrough the period.\n\nDetails: \n\nNorthwest winds and cold air advection look to continue overnight, \nwith the latter enhanced by passing weak shortwave within that \nbroader overhead troughing. Low level thermal profiles become more \nthan cold enough to entice a weak lake response, especially off Lake \nSuperior where boundary layer moisture profiles look a bit more \nrobust. This should drive at least the threat for a few light \nshowers into the famed lake effect areas of eastern upper Michigan. \nLikely to see at least a few clouds rotate off northern Lake \nMichigan...with this and maintenance of those northwest winds making \na tricky temperature forecast. Simple breadth of cold air advection \nsupports lows down into the 30s across interior areas. Borderline \nfrost-producing temperatures at best, and honestly wonder if frost \nwill form at all given those winds. Given uncertainties and such \nmarginal conditions feel best course of action is to forgo any \nspecific frost headline.\n\nProbably still a few lake clouds on Monday, especially north of the \nbridge, with cold thermal profiles likely supporting a shallow cu \nfield elsewhere. A bit chilly Monday, with highs in the 50s for \nmost...and a few lower 60s on the south side of the sunrise side \nwhere downslope warming will be maximized. Another tricky \ntemperature forecast for Monday night as high pressure builds into \nand eventually moves east of the Northwoods. Pattern recognitions \nsupports a rather rapid nocturnal temperature response with clearing \nskies and light/calm winds during the evening and early overnight. \nWeak southerly return flow and likely some increasing higher level \nclouds will likely slow or even reverse the temperature free-fall \nduring the early morning hours. Statistical guidance remains quite \nchilly, with lows well down into the 30s away from the big waters, \nand some upper 20s in our traditional ice-box locations. Will likely \neventually need a round of frost/freeze headlines for Monday night \nacross at least sections of northern lower Michigan (frost/freeze \nheadline season for eastern upper has been expired).\n\nNot much to talk about for the remainder of the week into next \nweekend. Again, strong support that any real chilly air will retreat \nquickly north, and any moisture rich airmass will remain to our \nsouth. Likely looking at temperatures running near to a bit above \nspecific normal values through the period. Passing waves may bring \nsome lower end shower chances at times, but by and large the week \nlooks like a dry one.",
